<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dutch Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Dutch colonial empire</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Dutch Empire was the overseas colonial and commercial empire of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, built mainly from the seventeenth century by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-west-india-company">Dutch West India Company</a>. Centred on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">East Indies</a>, it also included trading posts and colonies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, and it lasted in part into the twentieth century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dutch-empire.jpg" alt="Dutch Empire" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Miyamaki, Oren neu dag, Artem Karimov, Golradir · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prinsenvlag.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> rebel provinces declared independence from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/act-of-abjuration">Act of Abjuration</a> of 1581. In 1580 Philip II brought Portugal into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberian-union">Iberian Union</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, which closed all Portuguese markets to the United Provinces, and in 1595 the Dutch set sail to acquire Asian goods for themselves. By 1600 Dutch traders had penetrated the Asian spice trade but lacked capital, and in 1602 the States General merged several enterprises into the semi-state-owned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a>, the VOC, which was granted a monopoly over Asian trade. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-west-india-company">Dutch West India Company</a> followed in 1621 for the Atlantic, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> and the west coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> activities abroad were at first commercial ventures that controlled shipping routes through strategically placed outposts rather than large territories. By the mid-17th century the two companies exercised quasi-governmental powers to negotiate treaties, wage war and administer territory. The conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banda-islands">Banda Islands</a> from 1609 to 1621, also called the Banda genocide, followed a collapse in negotiations over the Dutch nutmeg monopoly; under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-pieterszoon-coen">Jan Pieterszoon Coen</a> massacres, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportations</a>, starvation and disease left the islands severely depopulated. The West India Company took Pernambuco from Portugal in 1630 and by 1650 controlled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trades</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-of-good-hope">Cape of Good Hope</a> was founded in 1652 as a victualling station.</p><p>Wars with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> between 1652 and 1784 cost many settlements, and the rise of the British East India Company weakened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> influence. After the French invasion of 1795 made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavian-republic">Batavian Republic</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> occupied Dutch colonies in Asia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>. After the Japanese occupation of 1942 to 1945 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a> became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a> became independent in 1975.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Aruba, Curaçao and Sint Maarten remain autonomous constituent countries of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Kingdom of the Netherlands</a>. Names of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> origin survive in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, among them Brooklyn, Harlem and Staten Island, and the Dutch Fort Zeelandia still stands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paramaribo">Paramaribo</a>. Between 1800 and 1950 Dutch engineers built roads, railways and irrigation systems in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, which became the material base of the colonial and later Indonesian state. Dutch is the official language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a>, Aruba, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonaire">Bonaire</a> and Curaçao, and Indonesian inherited many words from Dutch.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dutch colonial empire” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
